>e

The Sandwich Hut
A low hut with a wide shutter that folds down into a counter. This is where your
life happens. Shelves and a cool pit hold the ingredients, a chopping board sits
in the middle of the counter, and the knife hangs from its nail. The village
green is back to the west.

On the counter are a loaf of bread and a sandwich knife. In the cool pit are
some cured meat and some pickled roots. On the pantry shelf are some honey and
some herb curd.

Hob leans on the counter with the air of a man who has been waiting since before
the sun. 

>ask hob about lunch
"Same as always," says Hob. "Meat and sourberry, thick as you like. And no
pickle. I have views about pickle."

>take knife
Taken.

>put loaf on board
(first taking the loaf of bread off the counter)
You put the loaf of bread on the chopping board.

>put meat on board
(first taking the cured meat out of the cool pit)
You put the cured meat on the chopping board.

>put berries on board
(first taking the sourberries out of the wicker basket)
You put the sourberries on the chopping board.

>make sandwich
You slice, stack and press, with the unhurried competence of long practice. The
result is a sandwich of meat and sourberries, between two thick slabs of
Lamuellan bread.

>give sandwich to hob
(first taking the sandwich)
Hob takes it in both hands, weighs it like a newborn and bites. For a while he
is quite unavailable for conversation. "That," he says at last, "is why we keep
you." He pays in a coin of no known denomination, shakes your hand, and strolls
off into the morning, chewing. You slip the coin into your dressing-gown pocket;
you have never seen its like.

Far overhead, a thin whine builds, as of a mosquito with ambitions. Some minutes
later a child skids in to shout that the mail hopper has been, and has left a
parcel on the village green with the sandwich maker's name on it. Nobody on
Lamuella has ever received a parcel before. The hopper itself, you gather, has
landed on the meadow and is not yet sure it will leave.

