The pilot dozes against the skiff's flank, cap over his eyes. 

A mail skiff squats in the grass, its engine ticking as it cools. 

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>give sandwich to pilot
The pilot eats it in four bites, eyes closed, like a man receiving a sacrament.
"I'd fly you to the edge of the Galaxy for that," he says. "Well. Not the edge.
But somewhere." He taps the skiff's flank. "Whenever you're ready."

[The score has just gone up by eight points.]

>enter skiff

The pilot checks nothing in particular, says a word to the instrument panel, and
the world drops out from under you. There is a sensation like missing a step in
the dark, followed by another like finding it. For one long instant in the
middle of the jump you think of a girl who stepped into one of these and was not
on the other side; you grip the armrest until the feeling passes.

"Not long now," says Ford, mostly to himself.


The Skiff Cabin
The inside of the mail skiff, which is mostly instrument panel, with seats added
later as an afterthought. Beyond the glass there is the particular grey that
means a jump is in progress. You are not meant to look at it, and you do.

The pilot hunches over the controls, cap over his eyes, humming. 

Ford is here, restless, taking in the place as though pricing it. 

[The score has just gone up by three points.]

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

"Hub Station," says the pilot. "Mind the gap." There is no gap. You mind it
anyway.

Hub Station: Arrivals
A long, low arrivals hall under a ceiling of strip lights, with the sort of
carpet that was chosen to hide things. A kiosk stands in the middle of the
floor; a plant, a very old plant, waits in a pot beside it. A sign says QUEUE
HERE and points at the floor. Customs is to the east.

>e

Customs Hall
A pale green hall divided by a counter and a pane of glass, behind which Clerk
Gruba sits like a very large moth in a very small cupboard. A rack of forms
stands by the glass, with a pen on a chain beside it; an enormous sign says FORM
27B/6 OR NO ONE PASSES. There is a bin marked ITEMS TO DECLARE (it is full of
nothing). Arrivals is to the west and the concourse lies east, past Gruba.

Clerk Gruba sits behind the glass, framed like a portrait of someone who has
never been surprised. 

You can also see a Form 27B/6 here.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

The cube writes on its face, in neat capitals, before Gruba has so much as
looked up: FORM 27B/6. YOU WERE ABOUT TO ASK. A moment later, from behind the
glass, Gruba says exactly that, in exactly that tone.

>ask ford about cube
Ford looks at the cube for a long moment. "It told you what the clerk was going
to say," he says, slowly, and takes out a pocket notebook, which he does not
often do. "Before he'd said it. I'm writing that down. Don't tell it I wrote it
down."

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

