>e

The Lawn
The middle of the park, just inside the tape, with the bulk tin looming over you
like a landed apartment block. A door in its side stands open, and on the grass
in front of it two creatures sit at a folding table with a very large chart,
looking worried. The pub yard is west.

Two Grebulons are bent over a chart on a folding table, arguing in the voices of
people who have been doing it for a century. 

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

Tricia follows, taking in the place with a journalist's eye.

Random drifts in after you at a distance she clearly considers dignified.

>give sandwich to grebulons
The nearer Grebulon takes it, eyes it, passes it to the other, who takes a bite
and looks up with great emotion. "This," says the first, "is hospitality. We had
forgotten. We had a button, you know, and no idea what it was for."

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>ask grebulons about the button
"The button," says the nearer Grebulon, vaguely. "It is red. It is very large.
We were told to press it when the room was full. We don't remember what room. Or
what happens." A silence. "Everything, probably."

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

